<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vizier</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as vizierate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A vizier is a high minister or chief administrator serving a ruler, a title used in many Islamic states and applied by historians to the chief officials of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> the vizier headed the bureaucracy, and the Seljuk vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a> became the model of the powerful minister. The Ottoman grand vizier managed government and armies on behalf of the sultan, and viziers also played major roles in the Fatimid and Safavid states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vizier.jpg" alt="Vizier" width="308" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Muhammad_Shah_Qajar_and_his_Vizier_Haj_Mirza_Aghasi_MET_DP345140.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Barmakid family, Persian administrators who served the early caliphs and rose to the height of their power as <b>viziers</b> under Harun al-Rashid until their sudden fall in 803, exemplified this multicultural elite.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>There was no stable <b>vizierate</b>, no codified administrative law and little bureaucratic continuity between the Safavid structures he inherited and the Afsharid state he created.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The qadi of Kairouan was a Hanafi who endorsed the createdness of the Qur'an, and the emirs consistently favoured Iraqis as their higher-ranking judges, while the <b>viziers</b> were Malikis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Civil administration was run by a divan of mostly Persian <b>viziers</b> and secretaries, who kept registers, collected taxes and drafted correspondence in Persian.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…remarkable careers of personal state-building, that of Salah al-Din Yusuf ibn Ayyub, known in the West as Saladin, a Kurdish commander of exceptional political intelligence who transformed himself from a constrained <b>vizier</b> in Cairo into the master of the largest Muslim realm between the Nile and the Tigris.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Administration followed Abbasid models, run by capable <b>viziers</b> drawn from the secretarial class, such as Ibn al-Amid, vizier of Rukn al-Dawla, and al-Sahib ibn Abbad, vizier of Mu'ayyad al-Dawla and Fakhr al-Dawla at Ray.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
